{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CghJxrYYbTh4WZmqa4kQT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013tXaMKQjaR6pQTq6DP2YjR","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261103-220909__x5b1962/work/PLAN.md","limit":400},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7341,"cache_read_input_tokens":15013,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7341},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7a314112-273a-4f48-a672-e1ae4461860d","uuid":"c24481cc-7743-4545-8c1d-caedef385a2b","timestamp":"2026-11-04T05:09:14.212Z","request_id":"req_011CghJxrKeSEvdrfDREBYBW","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_013tXaMKQjaR6pQTq6DP2YjR":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261103-220909__x5b1962/work/PLAN.md","limit":400}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013tXaMKQjaR6pQTq6DP2YjR","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Dragon Quilt Builders\n2\t\n3\t*A build plan for an original block-building action RPG inspired by Dragon Quest Builders.*\n4\tWritten for the developers (human or AI) who will build the demo from `TASK.md` and this file alone.\n5\tPlan date: 2026-11-03. Engine: my-3D2dge handoff v0.85.2 (commit `4390a98`). AI Hub client: session.js 1.4.0 (ai-hub commit `f8835e48`).\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## 0. At a glance\n10\t\n11\t| | |\n12\t|---|---|\n13\t| **Title** | **Dragon Quilt Builders** (picked by RNG; see §1) |\n14\t| **Pitch** | The patchwork realm came apart at the seams when the dragon **Frayvern** pulled out its Golden Thread and swallowed everyone's *Knack*, the gift of making things. You are the last **Builder**. Break blocks, gather materials, get *Notions* (ideas for recipes), craft, and build rooms block by block. Win the hearts of quirky residents, raise the town of **Thimbleton**, defend it from a monster siege, and unpick the knotted boss **Gnarlgrim**. |\n15\t| **Genre** | Sandbox action RPG with block building, crafting and town building. Same family as the source: real-time combat and a story told in chapters. |\n16\t| **Perspective** | Third-person **isometric 2.5D** over a true voxel world. The camera turns in 90° steps (Q/E or the right stick) and zooms. **Three-quarter** is an optional view in Settings. Characters are the engine's 3D puppet rigs drawn as pixel art. Why iso: it shows two side faces and the top of every block, so 3D building reads clearly with a fixed camera, and it is the engine's native strength. |\n17\t| **Core loop** | Explore → break blocks and gather → get a Notion → craft at a station → build rooms and Patterns → residents move in and give Cheer → town levels up and unlocks more → the story pushes you into harder regions and finally a siege and a boss. |\n18\t| **Demo scope** | \"Chapter One: A Stitch in Time\", about 60–75 minutes for a new player. It has 5 residents, 6 regions, 8 room types, 30+ block and object types, 17 quests, a mini-boss, a siege with 3 waves and a final boss. After the credits, free play continues. A co-op **Quilting Bee** round (AI Hub shared world, 1–4 players) is also playable solo from the title. |\n19\t| **Deliverable** | One self-contained HTML file in this folder's root: `DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174740.html`. Dev files live in the subfolder of the same name. `exit-interview.json` sits beside the HTML. |\n20\t\n21\tThe source's distinctive elements, and what each becomes here (all names, characters, art, music and places are original):\n22\t\n23\t| Dragon Quest Builders | Dragon Quilt Builders |\n24\t|---|---|\n25\t| The dark lord took the power to build; you are the legendary Builder | Frayvern swallowed the *Knack*; only you can still make things. NPCs marvel at beds and doors (\"You *made* that? On purpose?!\") |\n26\t| A goddess guides you as a voice from the sky (Rubiss) | **Granny Gossamer**, the Great Quilter who stitched the world: a warm, scatterbrained voice in golden dialog boxes |\n27\t| Banner of Hope founds a base; base level rises | **Banner of Mending**: planting it stitches a glowing border on the ground. **Cheer** points raise the **Town Level** (1–5), which widens the border and unlocks recipes |\n28\t| Inspiration: touching new materials gives recipe ideas | **Notions** (\"You've got a notion!\"; *notions* is the sewing-shop word for haberdashery) |\n29\t| Rooms recognised from walls ≥2 high, a door and a light, then typed by their furniture | Same rule. 8 room recipes (Bedroom, Workshop, Kitchen, Mess Hall, Smithy, Guard Post, Snug, Quilters' Hall) |\n30\t| Blueprints that residents help build | **Patterns**: ghost-block layouts. Residents take materials from the Town Chest and place blocks too |\n31\t| Resident requests (the \"!\" over their heads), gratitude hearts | Requests with \"!\" balloons. **Cheer bubbles** (hearts) float over happy residents; walk through them to collect |\n32\t| Hunger, day/night, sleeping in beds | **Belly** meter, a 12-minute day, sleep through the night in a bed |\n33\t| Weapons double as block-breaking tools with tiers | Darning Stick → Cobble Club → Copper Bodkin → **Seam Ripper**, the only tool that cuts Black Knots |\n34\t| Base-defence sieges; bosses beaten with things you built | The Siege of Thimbleton (3 waves). Gnarlgrim can only be *Tangled* by the **Bobbin Ballistas** you build and fire |\n35\t| Teleport portals between areas | **Bobbin Portals** |\n36\t| Punny, warm, earnest localisation with quirky dialects | The same tone (§7.10, §6.15) |\n37\t| Online co-op building (DQB2) | Co-op **Quilting Bee** rounds in AI Hub sessions |\n38\t\n39\t---\n40\t\n41\t## 1. Title selection\n42\t\n43\tTASK.md asks for a playful riff on the source's name, picked by a real RNG from 50 silently brainstormed titles. Fifty riffs were written. None uses the trademark \"Dragon Quest\" verbatim. Examples of the kinds of riff: Wagon Quest Builders, Dragon Crest Bricklayers, Dragon Quarry Builders. One was picked with Python's `secrets.randbelow(50)`, which draws from OS entropy (`os.urandom`), not a seeded PRNG. Result: index 42, **\"Dragon Quilt Builders\"**.\n44\t\n45\tThe pick sets the whole art and writing direction: the world is a patchwork quilt. Every block is a fabric patch with stitched seams, the villain unravels things, tools are sewing tools, and puns are sewing puns.\n46\t\n47\tFile naming (fixed; do not improvise): GameTitle = `DragonQuiltBuilders`, SOURCE = `DragonQuestBuilders`.\n48\t- Dev folder: `./DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174740/`\n49\t- Deliverable: `./DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174740.html`\n50\t- Exit interview: `./exit-interview.json`\n51\t\n52\tOn-screen title (logo, `<title>`, About screen, AI Hub name): **Dragon Quilt Builders**.\n53\t\n54\t---\n55\t\n56\t## 2. Design pillars\n57\t\n58\t1. **Everything is a patch.** Each block is a 16-unit fabric square with stitched edges. Building feels like quilting: colourful, cosy and tactile. Breaking a block makes a satisfying *rrrip* and a burst of fabric scraps. Placing one makes a *stitch!* and a squash-in.\n59\t2. **You are the only one with the Knack.** NPCs cannot build. Every bed, door and lantern you make visibly changes their lives: they use your rooms, sleep, eat, cook, help, and thank you. Make that cause and effect loud: popups, hearts, jingles.\n60\t3. **Readable at ten feet.** Big chunky pixels (`res: 'ps1'` scaled to the TV), a few large UI elements, controller-first prompts, never dense web-style screens.\n61\t4. **Warm, punny, earnest.** Dragon Quest-style writing: short lines, exclamation marks, gentle jokes, distinct voices, and a silent hero.\n62\t5. **Data-driven and designer-ready.** Blocks, items, recipes, rooms, monsters, residents, quests, waves, songs and world stamps are all plain data tables. The building mechanic doubles as the level editor: dev mode exports any region you build as a stamp.\n63\t\n64\t---\n65\t\n66\t## 3. Deliverables, folder layout, commands\n67\t\n68\t```\n69\t./PLAN.md, ./TASK.md, ./STAGE.md                      (given)\n70\t./DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174740.html   (built; the game)\n71\t./exit-interview.json                                  (written at the end; parses as JSON)\n72\t./DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174740/\n73\t    engine/            copy of my-3d2dge dist/handoff v0.85.2 (my-3d2dge.js is modified, see §4.2)\n74\t    vendor/aihub/      session.js (unmodified client 1.4.0), harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (testing only)\n75\t    src/               game modules, concatenated in file-name order (see §5.2)\n76\t    src/index.template.html\n77\t    tools/build.mjs    bundles everything into the root HTML; also writes dev/index.html\n78\t    tools/hub-serve.mjs  serves a folder on 127.0.0.1:<OS port> for the hub harness (prints the port; kills only its own PID)\n79\t    tests/run.mjs      THE test command (see §11)\n80\t    tests/lib/*.mjs    headless loader, fake hub bus, autopilot\n81\t    tests/unit/*.test.mjs, tests/play/*.test.mjs, tests/net/*.test.mjs, tests/browser/*.mjs\n82\t    dev/index.html     loads engine + src files separately (debugging, file://)\n83\t    check-output/      screenshots and filmstrips (git-ignored)\n84\t```\n85\t\n86\tGetting the dependencies:\n87\t- Do not clone into `/tmp`, which is a small shared tmpfs and nearly full. Work inside the dev folder.\n88\t- Engine:\n89\t  ```\n90\t  git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git _eng\n91\t  cd _eng\n92\t  git sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\n93\t  ```\n94\t  Then copy `dist/handoff/*` into `engine/` and delete `_eng`.\n95\t- Hub: `curl -fsSO` the four files from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/` (session.js, harness.html, bridge.js, round.js) into `vendor/aihub/`. Read `lib/hub/README.md` (the protocol) once, whole.\n96\t- Inside `engine/`, run `npm install` (Playwright 1.63.0 is pinned) and `npx playwright install chromium`. Both are for the browser checks only.\n97\t\n98\tCommands (run from the dev folder):\n99\t\n100\t| Command | What it does |\n101\t|---|---|\n102\t| `node tools/build.mjs` | Writes the root HTML: `<meta charset>`, then **session.js inlined unmodified as the first script in `<head>`**, the hub meta tags, the boot splash, the engine, then `src/*.js` in order. Also writes `dev/index.html`. Fails on a missing file or a syntax error (`node --check` on each module first). |\n103\t| `node tests/run.mjs` | Every headless suite. Prints **only failures** and one final line: `N passed, M failed (12.3 s)`. Exit code 1 on failure. |\n104\t| `node tests/run.mjs --only voxel,rooms` | A subset (rerun only what the change covers). |\n105\t| `node tests/run.mjs --browser` | Adds the Playwright suites: the AI Hub harness checks and the screenshot capture. Still one line of output plus failures. |\n106\t| `node engine/tools/check.mjs <html>#<scene>` | The engine's browser checker (errors, look notes, snapshot). |\n107\t| `node engine/tools/filmstrip.mjs <html>#play --steps \"...\" --seed 1` | Frame-by-frame strip, for judging animations. |\n108\t\n109\tPorts and processes: `tools/hub-serve.mjs` runs `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` and parses the printed port. It fetches the game page and checks that its `<title>` contains \"Dragon Quilt Builders\" before trusting anything. It kills only the PID it spawned. Never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`. Prefer `file://` and headless Node everywhere else.\n110\t\n111\t---\n112\t\n113\t## 4. Tech stack\n114\t\n115\t### 4.1 Stack\n116\t- **my-3D2dge v0.85.2, full engine** (`engine/my-3d2dge.js`, which already contains every module: story, grid, songs, ...). Plain ES2020 JavaScript, no framework, no bundler beyond `tools/build.mjs` (string concatenation).\n117\t- **Canvas 2D pixel rendering** through the engine's renderer. `res: 'ps1'` (320×240) with `expand: true`, so a 16:9 TV shows about 427×240 game pixels at a whole-number scale. GPU lighting is not used (it requires a TileMap); canvas lighting is.\n118\t- **Audio:** the engine's chip synth and music sequencer, plus our own Web Audio formant voice synth (§6.17) on the engine's `AudioContext`.\n119\t- **AI Hub Session Protocol v1**, client 1.4.0, shared-world mode (§6.21).\n120\t- **Node 18+** for headless tests (`vm` contexts, as `engine/game/test.mjs` does). **Playwright** (pinned by the handoff) for the browser checks.\n121\t- No external runtime dependencies and no CDN. The HTML must work from `file://`.\n122\t- **Performance:** the budget is unlimited (TASK.md), so add detail, lights and effects freely. FPS targets and optimisation are out of scope. The atlas and culling design in §6.1 exists for correctness and a smooth feel, not as an optimisation target.\n123\t\n124\t### 4.2 Engine changes (keep a list; it goes into the About screen and the exit interview)\n125\tEdit `engine/my-3d2dge.js` directly, with a `// DQB:` comment on every change:\n126\t1. **Input:** an `input.wheelSteps()` accessor (whole notches this step, from `deltaY`) that works while `camera.wheel` is `false`, so the wheel can cycle the hotbar. A right-stick flick helper, `input.flick('x')`, which returns -1/0/1 once per push past 0.6 with a 0.25 s rearm.\n127\t2. **Dialog:** an `onLetter(ch, index, page)` hook called for each newly shown character (default: the old single blip). Our voice synth uses it per speaker.\n128\t3. **Audio:** a third bus, `audio._voiceBus` (master → voice), with `audio.voiceVolume` and a standard setting, \"Voices\" (0–100%).\n129\t4. **Humanoid weapons:** new weapon kinds `'stick'`, `'club'` and `'ripper'`, drawn next to the existing `'sword'`/`'staff'` code using the same blade/tip joints, so `rig.tip()`, smears and moves keep working.\n130\t5. **Glyph default:** `input.glyphs` defaults to `'playstation'` for this game. Our Settings item \"Button glyphs\" (PlayStation / Xbox) writes it.\n131\t6. **Body ceilings:** none in the engine; `VoxelBody` (§6.1.6) handles them in game code. If the renderer, Body or Input need anything else, add it the same way and log it.\n132\t\n133\tDo **not** rebuild the standard features: pause menu, settings, rebinding, full screen, boot screen, save slots. Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: {...} })` and `game.saves`.\n134\t\n135\t---\n136\t\n137\t## 5. Architecture\n138\t\n139\t### 5.1 Principles\n140\t- **One namespace.** `globalThis.DQ` holds every module. Each `src/NN-name.js` is an IIFE that adds to `DQ` and reads only what earlier files defined.\n141\t- **Logic and drawing are split.** Every system has `update(dt)` with no rendering, and a separate `draw(r)`. All logic runs headless in Node: `new E.Game()` is headless without a DOM and `draw` is skipped. UI screens are state machines (focus index, open tab, scroll), driven by input actions and testable headless; drawing reads that state.\n142\t- **Determinism.** No `Math.random` in game logic. Use named seeded streams, `DQ.rng(label)` (built on `E.rng(seed ^ hash(label))`). World seed `20261103`. In a hub session, shared streams come from `AIHub.rng(label)`. Cosmetic randomness (particles, weather) uses its own streams.\n143\t- **Event bus.** `DQ.events.on(name, fn)` and `emit(name, payload)` decouple systems. Quests, tutorials, the HUD, audio stingers, stats and tests all listen. Events:\n144\t  - Blocks and items: `blockBroken`, `blockPlaced`, `objectPlaced`, `objectRemoved`, `itemGained`, `itemUsed`, `crafted`, `notion`\n145\t  - Town: `roomFormed`, `roomLost`, `cheer`, `townLevel`, `patternPlaced`, `patternDone`\n146\t  - Residents and combat: `npcArrived`, `talk`, `monsterKilled`, `builderHurt`, `builderKO`\n147\t  - Story and time: `questStep`, `questDone`, `timeOfDay`, `siegeWave`, `bossPhase`, `chapterClear`\n148\t- **One context object.** `DQ.ctx = { game, world, builder, town, story, quests, npcs, monsters, ui, audioX, tuning, events, net, mode }` is created per play session. Systems receive it in their constructor and never reach for globals beyond `DQ`.\n149\t- **Data tables** live in `src/0x-data-*.js` as JSON-compatible literals: no functions in data except clearly marked `make` generators for stamps. Designers edit those files.\n150\t\n151\t### 5.2 Modules (`src/`, concatenated in this order)\n152\t\n153\t| File | Owns | Key API |\n154\t|---|---|---|\n155\t| `00-core.js` | `DQ` namespace, constants (`T=16`, world sizes), `hash`, `rng`, event bus, `DQ.ctx` factory, logging | `DQ.rng(label)`, `DQ.events` |\n156\t| `01-data-blocks.js` | Block registry (§7.2) | `DQ.BLOCKS[id]`, `DQ.blockId(key)` |\n157\t| `02-data-items.js` | Items, recipes, notions (§7.3–7.4) | `DQ.ITEMS`, `DQ.RECIPES` |\n158\t| `03-data-objects.js` | Placeable objects and their `E.SHAPES` entries (§7.5) | `DQ.OBJECTS` |\n159\t| `04-data-rooms.js` | Room recipes, town levels (§7.6–7.7) | `DQ.ROOMS`, `DQ.LEVELS` |\n160\t| `05-data-world.js` | Regions, stamps (ASCII layers), POIs, portals, plant scatter, spawn tables (§7.1) | `DQ.WORLD` |\n161\t| `06-data-npcs.js` | Residents: look, voice, schedule, job, dialog nodes (§7.8) | `DQ.NPCS` |\n162\t| `07-data-monsters.js` | Monster and boss stats, AI params, drops (§7.9) | `DQ.MONSTERS` |\n163\t| `08-data-quests.js` | Quests, steps, conditions, rewards, `E.Story` nodes, cutscene ids (§7.10) | `DQ.QUESTS`, `DQ.STORY_NODES` |\n164\t| `09-data-audio.js` | Songs, jingles, sfx voices, voice profiles (§7.11–7.12) | `DQ.SONGS`, `DQ.SFX`, `DQ.VOICES` |\n165\t| `09b-data-tuning.js` | Tuning defaults and slider metadata (§6.19) | `DQ.TUNING_DEFS` |\n166\t| `10-voxel-world.js` | Voxel storage, edits, queries, collision, raycast, save diff | `DQ.VoxelWorld` (§6.1) |\n167\t| `11-voxel-render.js` | Face-sprite atlas, column-run rendering, cutaway, water, void fringe, overlays | `DQ.VoxelRenderer` |\n168\t| `12-worldgen.js` | Terrain, layers, ores, trees, water, stamps, plants, portals | `DQ.generateWorld(world, def)` |\n169\t| `13-physics.js` | `VoxelBody` (ceilings, auto-hop, water, void), stair ramps | `DQ.VoxelBody` |\n170\t| `14-input.js` | Action presets, intents, cursor modes, mouse picking, glyph icons and prompt strings | `DQ.Controls`, `DQ.Glyphs` |\n171\t| `15-builder.js` | Player entity: stats, equipment, actions (swing, break, place, use, eat, interact), KO and respawn | `DQ.Builder` |\n172\t| `16-inventory.js` | Bag, hotbar, chests, Town Chest, equipment, stacks, overflow | `DQ.Bag`, `DQ.Chest` |\n173\t| `17-crafting.js` | Stations, recipe availability, crafting, notion unlocks | `DQ.Crafting` |\n174\t| `18-objects.js` | Object instances: doors, lights, beds, stations, traps, ballistas, portals, plants (regrowth) | `DQ.Objects` |\n175\t| `19-rooms.js` | Room detection and classification, room registry | `DQ.Rooms` |\n176\t| `20-town.js` | Banner, border, Cheer, town level, Patterns (ghosts, progress, helpers) | `DQ.Town` |\n177\t| `21-nav.js` | Voxel navigation graph, A*, flow fields, breaker paths | `DQ.Nav` |\n178\t| `22-npcs.js` | Resident AI: schedule, needs, jobs, helping, fighting, Cheer bubbles | `DQ.Residents` |\n179\t| `23-monsters.js` | Monster AI, spawning, despawning | `DQ.Monsters` |\n180\t| `24-combat.js` | Damage, hit effects, knockback, i-frames, drops, the Lost Bundle | `DQ.Combat`, `DQ.Drops` |\n181\t| `25-siege.js` | Siege director (prep, waves, fail/retry), boss directors | `DQ.Siege` |\n182\t| `26-bosses.js` | Sir Buttonsworth, Gnarlgrim, Tangle mechanics | `DQ.Bosses` |\n183\t| `27-time.js` | Day clock, lighting by hour, weather, sleep | `DQ.Clock` |\n184\t| `28-story.js` | Quest director (steps on events), `E.Story` wrapper, cutscene scripts, objective targets | `DQ.Quests`, `DQ.Cutscenes` |\n185\t| `29-ui-hud.js` | HUD, prompts, popups (Notion, Room, Level), Cheer, damage numbers, objective marker, cursor | `DQ.Hud` |\n186\t| `30-ui-menus.js` | Title, name entry, Satchel (Bag/Map/Journal), crafting, chest, travel, banner, dialog skin, results, credits, About | `DQ.Menus` |\n187\t| `31-audio.js` | Music director (per region, time and town level layers), sfx defs, Animalese voice synth | `DQ.AudioX` |\n188\t| `32-save.js` | `game.saves` read/write, versioning, migrate, autosave | `DQ.Save` |\n189\t| `33-dev.js` | Dev mode panel, cheats, sliders, overlays, stamp export | `DQ.Dev` |\n190\t| `34-attract.js` | Attract-mode director (vignettes, AI builder, cheats) | `DQ.Attract` |\n191\t| `35-ai.js` | Shared AI \"autopilot\" (walkTo, breakAt, placeAt, craft, talk, fight). Used by attract, tests and Bee bots | `DQ.Autopilot` |\n192\t| `36-net.js` | AI Hub session flow, message codec, host and client logic, loopback | `DQ.Net` |\n193\t| `37-bee.js` | Quilting Bee rules (hub round and solo challenge), its world, scoring | `DQ.Bee` |\n194\t| `38-scenes.js` | Scenes: title, attract, newgame, intro, play, results, credits, bee, hubcard | `DQ.Scenes` |\n195\t| `99-main.js` | Creates `E.Game`, settings, menu, boot steps, `game.debug`, starts | — |\n196\t\n197\t### 5.3 Game object and scenes\n198\t```js\n199\tconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'iso', views: ['iso', 'threequarter'],\n200\t  input: DQ.Controls.PLAY, id: 'dragon-quilt-builders',\n201\t  menu: { saves: { slots: 3, version: 1, migrate: DQ.Save.migrate },\n202\t          items: [['Satchel', openSatchel], ['Dev Tools', DQ.Dev.open], ['About & Credits', openAbout]],   // Dev Tools hidden in a hub session\n203\t          settings: DQ.SETTINGS, controls: DQ.Controls.NAMES, quit: 'title' } });\n204\t```\n205\t- **Scenes:** `title` (menu and attract countdown), `attract`, `newgame` (name, look), `intro` (story crawl), `play` (`pausable: true`, `camera: { zoom: false }`: the engine's continuous player zoom stays off, because our own zoom steps call `game.setZoom`, §6.1.3), `results` (chapter clear), `credits`, `about` (also openable over the pause menu), `bee` (Quilting Bee, solo or hub), `hubcard` (the game's own AI Hub card).\n206\t- **Boot steps** (`game.start({ boot: { title: 'DRAGON QUILT BUILDERS', steps } })`):\n207\t  1. \"Unrolling the quilt\" (world generation)\n208\t  2. \"Threading the needles\" (face-sprite atlas, icons)\n209\t  3. \"Waking the residents\" (nav graph)\n210\t  4. \"Tuning the voices\" (audio definitions)\n211\t\n212\t  Boot messages: \"Counting the buttons\", \"Untangling yarn\", \"Ironing the seams\".\n213\t- **`play.update(dt)` order:**\n214\t  1. `Controls.intent()`\n215\t  2. `Builder.update`\n216\t  3. block actions\n217\t  4. `Objects.update` (doors, traps, ballistas, plants)\n218\t  5. `Residents.update`\n219\t  6. `Monsters.update`\n220\t  7. `Combat.resolve`\n221\t  8. `Drops.update`\n222\t  9. `Town.update` (rooms debounce, Cheer, Patterns)\n223\t  10. `Quests.update`\n224\t  11. `Clock.update` (spawns, weather)\n225\t  12. `Siege.update`\n226\t  13. camera (`game.focus` on the builder with lead)\n227\t  14. `Hud.update`\n228\t\n229\t  A modal UI (dialog, menu, cutscene) short-circuits gameplay updates. The world still animates (particles, water) and the clock pauses.\n230\t- **`play.draw(r)` order:**\n231\t  1. void background\n232\t  2. `VoxelRenderer.queue(r)` (sets `r._map`)\n233\t  3. objects (`r.shape`)\n234\t  4. Pattern ghosts\n235\t  5. town border stitches\n236\t  6. drops\n237\t  7. actors (builder with `xray`, residents, monsters)\n238\t  8. cursor\n239\t  9. lights (`game.lights.add`)\n240\t  10. `r.overlay` (HUD, popups, dialog, menus)\n241\t- **`game.debug`:**\n242\t  - `snapshot()` returns `{ scene, day, hour, builder: {x,y,z,hp,maxHp,belly,equip}, bag: {id:n}, town: {level, cheer, border, rooms:[type]}, quests: {id: step|'done'}, residents: [{id,x,y,state}], monsters: n, siege: {phase, wave}, boss: {id, hp, phase}, errors: n }`.\n243\t  - Helpers: `teleport(x,y,z)`, `give(id,n)`, `flag(k,v)`, `beat(id)` (jump to a story beat), `world` (the VoxelWorld), `ctx`.\n244\t\n245\t---\n246\t## 6. Systems\n247\t\n248\t### 6.1 Voxel world (the riskiest system; built first, see §9 MS1)\n249\t\n250\tThe engine's `TileMap` is a heightmap: one wall type per cell, no passages under things. Building needs real 3D blocks (roofs, bridges, tunnels, mixed-material walls), so the game has its own `VoxelWorld`. It plugs into the engine where the engine expects a map.\n251\t\n252\t#### 6.1.1 Data\n253\t- Block size `T = 16` world units (one tile). Axes: x east, y south, z up.\n254\t- Story world: `W = 144, D = 144` columns, `H = 24` levels. Bee world: `48 × 48 × 16`.\n255\t- `vox: Uint8Array(W*D*H)`, column-major: `idx(x,y,z) = (y*W + x)*H + z`, so a column is contiguous.\n256\t- `meta: Map<idx, {rot, hp}>` holds stair rotation and partial damage. Damage heals back after 4 s without hits.\n257\t- `colVer: Uint32Array(W*D)` holds per-column versions for render caches. `topZ: Uint8Array(W*D)` is the highest solid level + 1, kept on edit.\n258\t- **Objects** (furniture, doors, lights, plants) are *not* voxels. They live in `DQ.Objects` with an anchor cell `(x,y,z)` and occupy that cell (doors and portals occupy 2 levels). `world.objAt(x,y,z)` is indexed by `Map<idx, obj>`.\n259\t- Block properties from `DQ.BLOCKS`: `solid`, `opaque`, `liquid`, `hp`, `tier`, `drop`, `tex`, `light`, `shape` (`'cube' | 'stair'`), `placeable`.\n260\t- **Level 0 is Backing Cloth** (unbreakable). Below and outside the world is the **Unravel**, a void. Anything falling below z = −16 (through a void hole or off the world's edge) respawns at its last safe position after a 0.6 s \"unravel\" effect. The builder takes 4 damage.\n261\t\n262\t#### 6.1.2 API (`DQ.VoxelWorld`)\n263\t```\n264\tget(x,y,z) → id            set(x,y,z,id,{by, silent}) → bool (emits blockPlaced/blockBroken, bumps colVer of the column and 4 neighbours)\n265\tsolidAt(x,y,z)  opaqueAt(x,y,z)  liquidAt(x,y,z)   (integer cells; outside the world and below level 0 is void, which reads as air)\n266\ttopZ(x,y)                  standLevels(x,y) → levels L where solid(L-1) and air(L), air(L+1)\n267\tgroundAt(px,py,r,z,step)   highest solid top ≤ z+step under the circle footprint, −Infinity over void (world units; feeds E.Body-style physics)\n268\tceilingAt(px,py,r,z)       lowest solid bottom above z within the footprint (head bumps)\n269\tcollide(body)              push a circle out of blocks overlapping [z+step, z+h) (same algorithm as TileMap.collide, per level)\n270\traycast(o,d,maxT) → {x,y,z, nx,ny,nz, t} | null          3D DDA (Amanatides–Woo) through cells\n271\tpickScreen(sx,sy) → {x,y,z, n:[nx,ny,nz], obj} | null     view ray through the screen pixel (see 6.1.5)\n272\tbounds(view) → {x0,y0,x1,y1}                             camera clamp (as TileMap.bounds)\n273\tT, solids=[] , _liftOrder(view,x,y,z,k)                  the renderer's hooks (see 6.1.4)\n274\tdiff() → [[idx, id], ...]   applyDiff(list)               save and network: cells that differ from the generated base world\n275\thash() → u32                                              tests and net convergence\n276\t```\n277\tGeneration writes `base` (a copy of `vox` taken after `generateWorld`), so `diff()` is a cheap scan. Saves store only the diff (typically under 30 KB).\n278\t\n279\t#### 6.1.3 Rendering: face-sprite blitting per block, queued per column run\n280\t- **Face atlas.** For each `(view id, zoom step)` the renderer pre-renders a small sprite for every material and visible face: `top`, `faceA` (the camera-facing side on the viewer's left) and `faceB` (right). In three-quarter view there is `top` and `front`. Each material gets 4 variants (picked by `hash(x,y,z) & 3`).\n281\t  - Sprites are rasterized per pixel with `E.tones(base)`. The pattern functions are in §8.1. Each face is an exact parallelogram of the projected block face, snapped to whole pixels. All blocks of a view and zoom project to the same shapes, so one atlas serves the whole world. It is rebuilt only when the zoom step changes. A 90° turn keeps iso faces identical, and the renderer only swaps which world face maps to A/B.\n282\t  - Light: top = `base`, faceA = `sh`, faceB = `deep`, following the engine's \"light from the viewer's upper left\".\n283\t- **Zoom steps.** Zoom is quantized to `[0.75, 1, 1.25, 1.5, 2]` (default 1). Our own zoom actions (− / =, right stick ↑/↓) step through them with `game.setZoom(step)`. The play scene sets `camera: { zoom: false }`, so the engine's continuous player zoom stays off and the atlas only ever needs these five scales. The chosen step is remembered in our settings (`dqZoom`).\n284\t- **Run queueing.** For each visible column, split its opaque solid blocks into runs `[z0, z1)`. Queue each run once:\n285\t  ```js\n286\t  r.queue(cx*16+8, cy*16+8, z0*16, g => drawRun(g, r, cx, cy, z0, z1), { occluder: true, top: z1*16 })\n287\t  ```\n288\t  `drawRun` blits, bottom to top, each block's visible faces: a side face shows where the neighbour column is not opaque at that level, and the top face shows where the block above is not opaque or is cut away. Then come overlays: crack stages on damaged blocks, contact AO (a 2-px dark band on side faces at a run's bottom where it meets the ground), top-face edge AO (a dark band along an edge whose neighbour is one level higher), and sun-shadow tint on tops that have a taller column within 2 cells toward the light. Overlays use `px.blend(g, a, 'multiply', ...)` with shared per-view overlay sprites.\n289\t- **Visible columns.** Invert the four screen corners to the ground plane at `z = 0` and at `z = H*16`. Take the cell bounding box plus 2 cells of margin, then clamp it. That is about 500–700 columns at zoom 1.\n290\t- **Liquids.** Water blocks are queued separately: `r.queue(..., z*16, drawWater)`. The water top is drawn with `px.blend(.7)` and a 4-frame shimmer, plus side faces next to air. Blocks under water get a blue multiply tint.\n291\t- **Void and fringe.** Behind the world, draw a deep-navy gradient (`r.sky(['#06051a','#0d0b2a','#141238'])`) with drifting cream \"lint motes\" and a faint stitched grid. Edge columns and void holes get frayed fringe threads: 1-px lines hanging 1–2 blocks below the lowest block, swaying with time. These are queued just before their column.\n292\t- **Cutaway** (seeing inside roofs, tunnels and canopies). Each frame, set `cut = heroLevel + 2` when an opaque block sits at a level in `[heroLevel+2, heroLevel+6]` in either of these places:\n293\t  - the hero's own column;\n294\t  - any of the 3 columns toward the camera. Step from the hero's cell by `(round(view.fx), round(view.fy))`, the ground direction that points at the camera; in iso both components are ±1.\n295\t\n296\t  Ordinary 2-high room walls (levels `heroLevel` and `heroLevel+1`) are never cut; the x-ray silhouette shows the hero behind them.\n297\t\n298\t  Blocks at `z ≥ cut` within radius 6.5 cells of the hero are skipped. A 1.5-cell band at the edge is drawn every other pixel (`px.polyDither`). A block just below the cut with a block above it gets a \"cross-section\" overlay: a darker top with a diagonal stitch hatch. The cut eases in and out over 0.2 s (the radius grows from 0). It stays on for 0.5 s after the hero leaves cover (hysteresis), so it does not flicker at doorways. Dev toggle: \"Cutaway: auto / always / off\".\n299\t- **X-ray.** The builder, remote players and bosses use `r.actor(..., { xray: { color, actors: false } })`. They show as tinted silhouettes behind blocks (the engine does this through `occluder` queue items).\n300\t- **Draw cost guard.** No baking hitches: blits are cheap. If a frame queues more than 2,500 runs (extreme zoom-out), draw the far columns' top faces only.\n301\t\n302\t#### 6.1.4 Depth sorting\n303\tThe renderer sorts by `view.order(x,y,z) = fx*x + fy*y + z*1e-3`. Runs in one column sort bottom to top, and columns sort by depth. For things that stand on blocks or sit under overhangs, the renderer calls `r._map._liftOrder(view, x, y, z, k)` for every queued item above `z > .5`. `VoxelRenderer.queue` sets `r._map = world`. Call it before queuing any actor or object: the renderer reads `r._map` at queue time and resets it to null at the start of every frame. Implement both directions:\n304\t- **Lift.** If the item's cell has a run whose top is `≤ z+1` and it is the highest such run, return `max(k, order(center, run.z0*16) + .002 + z*1e-5)`.\n305\t- **Sink.** If the item's cell has a run with `z0*16 ≥ z + 20` (an overhang above its head), return `min(k, order(center, thatRun.z0*16) - .002)`.\n306\t\n307\tAlso provide `world.T = 16` and `world.solids = []` (the renderer reads both). Test with a diorama (§11.2): a hero under a roof, on a bridge with a body below, beside and behind 2- and 3-high walls, on stairs, and in a doorway. Write a filmstrip check in all 4 rotations.\n308\t\n309\t#### 6.1.5 Picking (mouse and touch-free targeting)\n310\tUse the line of sight through a screen pixel, as `TileMap.pick` computes it: P and N from the view rows. Clip to the world box and run the 3D DDA from the camera side. The first opaque or solid block hit, with the entry face normal, is the **break target**. `hit + normal` is the **place target**. Objects take part through `world.objAt` (placed objects count as hit targets, so the mouse can click a bench or a bed). Actors are picked with the engine's `{ pick: entity }` on `r.actor`/`r.shape` and `r.pick(sx, sy)` (the last frame's draws). Actor picks win over blocks when both are under the pointer. Respect the cutaway: cut blocks are not pickable.\n311\t\n312\t#### 6.1.6 Physics (`DQ.VoxelBody extends E.Body`)\n313\t- Builder body: `r = 5, h = 28` (fits under a 2-block ceiling), `eye = 22, chest = 16`, `step = 2`, `gravity = 800`.\n314\t- **`update(dt, world)`.** Run `E.Body.update` (it calls `world.collide` and `world.groundAt`). Then check the ceiling: if `vz > 0` and `z + h > ceilingAt(...)`, clamp `z` and set `vz = 0`.\n315\t  - **Stairs:** in a stair cell the ground is a ramp along the stair's `rot` (0 → 16 over the cell), so walking up stairs needs no jump.\n316\t  - **Water:** in water, speed ×0.55. A column with water 2 or more levels deep blocks walkers like a wall (bodies cannot enter, and a falling body is pushed out to `lastSafe`). The builder cannot swim, as in the source.\n317\t- **Auto-hop** (setting, on by default). Moving into a 1-block ledge with 2 free levels above it, on the ground, for over 0.08 s triggers `jump(215)` with a small forward boost.\n318\t- **Safe positions.** Store `lastSafe` every 0.5 s while standing on solid ground that is not near a void edge.\n319\t- **No block may be placed inside a body.** `Builder.place` checks every builder, resident and monster AABB first, then shows \"Something's in the way!\"\n320\t- **`world.escape(body)`** moves a buried body to the nearest standable cell within 4 cells. It runs after any set() that could bury something (worldgen stamps, Tangle blocks).\n321\t\n322\t### 6.2 Camera and views\n323\t- `game.follow(builder.body, { z: 14, lead: 18 })`, with `game.cam.bounds = v => world.bounds(v)` and `cam.smooth = .12`.\n324\t- **Rotation:**\n325\t  - Q/E keys, or a right-stick flick (`input.flick('x')`), turn by ±90° with `game.rotateView(±90)`.\n326\t  - Cover the snap with a 0.18 s crossfade: grab the previous frame's buffer before turning and blend it out with `px.blend` on the overlay. Play a soft \"whoosh\" (`sfx('whoosh', {vol:.2})`).\n327\t  - Movement keeps working in screen space (`view.screenDirToGround`).\n328\t- **Zoom:** right stick ↑/↓ or `−`/`=` step through the zoom levels (§6.1.3). In play the mouse wheel belongs to the hotbar; it zooms only on the Map screen. R3/R resets rotation and zoom (`game.resetCamera()`, then zoom step 1).\n329\t- **Settings → Camera view:** Isometric (default) or Three-quarter. It switches with `game.setView`; the atlas has entries for both.\n330\t- Cutscenes take the camera with tweens on a proxy focus object and give it back on exit.\n331\t\n332\t### 6.3 The Builder (player)\n333\t- **Look:** chosen at New Game. Look A: spiky hair and a bandana. Look B: ponytail and an apron. Four palette swatches. Name entry (default \"Patch\") on an on-screen letter grid that works with a pad, keys or mouse; typing letters works directly. The builder never speaks (Dragon Quest tradition); choices are shown as menu options.\n334\t- **Rig:** `new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.0, build: 'chibi', ...look })`. Its head top must stay ≤ 28 units; assert in a test via `rig.worldOffset` of the head joint.\n335\t- **Stats:**\n336\t  - HP 40 (+10 per Heartpatch, ×3 in the story, so 70 max). Belly 100. Base attack 4.\n337\t  - Equipment slots: weapon, body, shield.\n338\t  - No experience levels. Growth comes from Heartpatches, gear and the town (as in DQB1).\n339\t- **Movement (tunable):** walk 78, sprint ×1.45 (Belly drains ×1.5), accel 900, friction 10 when no input, jump 215, air control 0.6, coyote 0.08 s, jump buffer 0.12 s.\n340\t- **Swing** (□ / LMB / J), using the equipped weapon:\n341\t  - A tap swings a 2-hit combo (`E.Combo(['slash','backslash'], { window: .3 })`): 0.38 s per swing, active from 0.35 to 0.6 of it.\n342\t  - Each swing hits monsters in a 70° arc within 22 units (`E.inArc` with `dz` ±14).\n343\t  - If no monster is in the arc, the swing hits the **break target**:\n344\t    1. It deals the weapon's `blockDmg`; the block's HP is `DQ.BLOCKS[id].hp`.\n345\t    2. Crack overlays show at 33%, 66% and 90%.\n346\t    3. At 0 HP the block breaks: fabric-scrap particles in its tones, a *rrrip* sfx, and the drop spawns.\n347\t    4. Blocks above a tool's tier go *clink*: sparks, no damage, and the prompt \"Too tough for your Darning Stick!\"\n348\t- **Spin Stitch** (unlocked in M5): hold swing for 0.6 s. A charge glow and rising tone play. Release for a 360° `spin` move: 1.5× damage, knockback 180, radius 26. It does not break blocks.\n349\t- **Place** (△ / RMB / K), using the selected hotbar item:\n350\t  - **Block:** set at the place target, if it is in reach (4.5 blocks from chest to cell centre), empty, not inside a body, and inside the world. The block pops in with a squash tween (scale 0.6 → 1.08 → 1 over 0.12 s), a stitch spark and a \"stitch\" sfx.\n351\t  - **Object:** placed with facing = the builder's facing, snapped to 90°. A green or red ghost preview shows validity.\n352\t  - **Usable** (food, Heartpatch, Banner): use it.\n353\t  - A full hotbar is fine; place takes one item from the bag stack.\n354\t- **Interact** (○ / F / RMB on an NPC or object): talk, open a chest, use a station, sleep, man a ballista, travel by portal, pick up the Lost Bundle, plant the Banner (on the marked spot).\n355\t- **Eat:** use food from the hotbar or bag. Belly and HP restore (§7.3), with a munch ×3 animation.\n356\t- **Hurt:** i-frames 0.8 s, knockback 90, flash, `expr: 'wince'`, a rumble of .35.\n357\t- **KO** at 0 HP:\n358\t  1. The `'die'` pose plays, then an *unravel* effect: the rig dissolves into thread particles.\n359\t  2. Granny says \"Oh, dearie, you've come undone!\"\n360\t  3. Fade, then respawn at the Banner (or the start point before it exists) with full HP and Belly 60.\n361\t  4. A **Lost Bundle** stays at the KO spot (map marker) holding 50% (rounded down) of each material stack. Tools and key items are never lost. On Relaxed, nothing is lost. During the siege or boss, KO instead retries from the siege checkpoint (§6.12).\n362\t- **Belly** drains 1 point every 9 s of real time (100 → 0 in 15 minutes); sprint ×1.5, fighting ×1.2. At 0, HP drops 1 every 4 s. \"Your belly is rumbling!\" warnings show at 25% and 10%.\n363\t\n364\t### 6.4 Input, controls, prompts (controller first, keyboard and mouse native)\n365\t\n366\tAction presets (`DQ.Controls.PLAY`, `DQ.Controls.MENU`) are passed to `game.input.use(...)`. The engine keeps the d-pad and arrow keys on movement and menus. The tables below are the defaults; players rebind through the standard Controls screen (`menu.controls` names them).\n367\t\n368\t| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Xbox glyphs | Keyboard / mouse |\n369\t|---|---|---|---|\n370\t| Move | Left stick / D-pad | LS / D-pad | WASD / arrows |\n371\t| Camera turn 90° | Right stick ←/→ (flick) | RS | Q / E |\n372\t| Zoom | Right stick ↑/↓ | RS | − / = |\n373\t| Reset camera | R3 | RS click | R |\n374\t| Jump (menus: confirm) | ✕ | A | Space (menus: Enter/Space) |\n375\t| Swing / break (hold: Spin Stitch) | □ | X | Left click (keyboard-only: J) |\n376\t| Place / use item | △ | Y | Right click (keyboard-only: K) |\n377\t| Talk / interact (menus: back) | ○ | B | F (menus: Esc/Backspace) |\n378\t| Hotbar previous / next | L1 / R1 | LB / RB | Wheel, 1–8, [ / ] |\n379\t| Sprint (hold; toggle in Settings) | R2 | RT | Left Shift |\n380\t| Builder's Eye (precision cursor, hold) | L2 | LT | C |\n381\t| Satchel (Bag, Map, Journal) | Create | View | Tab / I (Bag), M (Map), N (Journal) |\n382\t| Map shortcut | Touchpad click (button 17, if reported) | — | M |\n383\t| Pause menu | Options | Menu | Esc / P |\n384\t\n385\t- **Cursor modes** (`Controls.cursorMode`):\n386\t  - **Auto** (stick or keys used last). The target cell is the neighbour cell in the builder's facing, rounded to 8 directions.\n387\t    - Break target: the first solid block in that cell at level L0 (feet), then L0+1, then L0−1.\n388\t    - Place target: L0−1 if it is empty (fills holes), else L0, else L0+1, else L0+2. So walls 3 high can be stacked from the ground and pits get filled.\n389\t  - **Mouse** (the mouse moved or clicked in the last 2 s). The target follows `world.pickScreen` under the pointer. LMB swings at that block and RMB places there. The builder turns to face the target.\n390\t  - **Builder's Eye** (hold L2 / C). The builder stands still and a precision cursor appears at the auto target.\n391\t    - Stick, d-pad or WASD step the cursor one cell (repeat .16 s), clamped to reach.\n392\t    - R1 or Space raises the cursor one level; R2 or Shift lowers it.\n393\t    - Swing and place act at the cursor.\n394\t    - On screen: a grid of stitched dots around the cursor and a level number.\n395\t- **Cursor display:** a stitched wireframe cube (12 projected edges, 1-px dashed cream thread, pulsing). The break target highlights the block's faces. The place target shows a 50%-alpha ghost of the held block or object. Out of reach, it turns red and dashed, with \"Too far\".\n396\t- **Glyph icons** (`DQ.Glyphs.draw(g, actionOrCode, x, y)`). The engine font lacks ✕○□△, so the game draws its own 9×9 pixel icons:\n397\t  - PlayStation: ✕ blue `#7aa7ff`, ○ red `#ff6a6a`, □ pink `#ff8ad8`, △ green `#55dca8`, on dark discs.\n398\t  - Xbox: A green, B red, X blue, Y yellow letters on discs.\n399\t  - L1/R1/L2/R2/LB/RB/LT/RT as rounded rectangles. Sticks as rings with L or R. Options/Create as pills.\n400\t  - Keyboard keycaps are rounded light-grey rectangles with the key's label from `input.label()`; Space is wide."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7a314112-273a-4f48-a672-e1ae4461860d","uuid":"dab92cd4-0eb1-4d62-8208-41afd91d1866","timestamp":"2026-11-04T05:09:14.352Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261103-220909__x5b1962/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Dragon Quilt Builders\n\n*A build plan for an original block-building action RPG inspired by Dragon Quest Builders.*\nWritten for the developers (human or AI) who will build the demo from `TASK.md` and this file alone.\nPlan date: 2026-11-03. Engine: my-3D2dge handoff v0.85.2 (commit `4390a98`). AI Hub client: session.js 1.4.0 (ai-hub commit `f8835e48`).\n\n---\n\n## 0. At a glance\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Title** | **Dragon Quilt Builders** (picked by RNG; see §1) |\n| **Pitch** | The patchwork realm came apart at the seams when the dragon **Frayvern** pulled out its Golden Thread and swallowed everyone's *Knack*, the gift of making things. You are the last **Builder**. Break blocks, gather materials, get *Notions* (ideas for recipes), craft, and build rooms block by block. Win the hearts of quirky residents, raise the town of **Thimbleton**, defend it from a monster siege, and unpick the knotted boss **Gnarlgrim**. |\n| **Genre** | Sandbox action RPG with block building, crafting and town building. Same family as the source: real-time combat and a story told in chapters. |\n| **Perspective** | Third-person **isometric 2.5D** over a true voxel world. The camera turns in 90° steps (Q/E or the right stick) and zooms. **Three-quarter** is an optional view in Settings. Characters are the engine's 3D puppet rigs drawn as pixel art. Why iso: it shows two side faces and the top of every block, so 3D building reads clearly with a fixed camera, and it is the engine's native strength. |\n| **Core loop** | Explore → break blocks and gather → get a Notion → craft at a station → build rooms and Patterns → residents move in and give Cheer → town levels up and unlocks more → the story pushes you into harder regions and finally a siege and a boss. |\n| **Demo scope** | \"Chapter One: A Stitch in Time\", about 60–75 minutes for a new player. It has 5 residents, 6 regions, 8 room types, 30+ block and object types, 17 quests, a mini-boss, a siege with 3 waves and a final boss. After the credits, free play continues. A co-op **Quilting Bee** round (AI Hub shared world, 1–4 players) is also playable solo from the title. |\n| **Deliverable** | One self-contained HTML file in this folder's root: `DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174740.html`. Dev files live in the subfolder of the same name. `exit-interview.json` sits beside the HTML. |\n\nThe source's distinctive elements, and what each becomes here (all names, characters, art, music and places are original):\n\n| Dragon Quest Builders | Dragon Quilt Builders |\n|---|---|\n| The dark lord took the power to build; you are the legendary Builder | Frayvern swallowed the *Knack*; only you can still make things. NPCs marvel at beds and doors (\"You *made* that? On purpose?!\") |\n| A goddess guides you as a voice from the sky (Rubiss) | **Granny Gossamer**, the Great Quilter who stitched the world: a warm, scatterbrained voice in golden dialog boxes |\n| Banner of Hope founds a base; base level rises | **Banner of Mending**: planting it stitches a glowing border on the ground. **Cheer** points raise the **Town Level** (1–5), which widens the border and unlocks recipes |\n| Inspiration: touching new materials gives recipe ideas | **Notions** (\"You've got a notion!\"; *notions* is the sewing-shop word for haberdashery) |\n| Rooms recognised from walls ≥2 high, a door and a light, then typed by their furniture | Same rule. 8 room recipes (Bedroom, Workshop, Kitchen, Mess Hall, Smithy, Guard Post, Snug, Quilters' Hall) |\n| Blueprints that residents help build | **Patterns**: ghost-block layouts. Residents take materials from the Town Chest and place blocks too |\n| Resident requests (the \"!\" over their heads), gratitude hearts | Requests with \"!\" balloons. **Cheer bubbles** (hearts) float over happy residents; walk through them to collect |\n| Hunger, day/night, sleeping in beds | **Belly** meter, a 12-minute day, sleep through the night in a bed |\n| Weapons double as block-breaking tools with tiers | Darning Stick → Cobble Club → Copper Bodkin → **Seam Ripper**, the only tool that cuts Black Knots |\n| Base-defence sieges; bosses beaten with things you built | The Siege of Thimbleton (3 waves). Gnarlgrim can only be *Tangled* by the **Bobbin Ballistas** you build and fire |\n| Teleport portals between areas | **Bobbin Portals** |\n| Punny, warm, earnest localisation with quirky dialects | The same tone (§7.10, §6.15) |\n| Online co-op building (DQB2) | Co-op **Quilting Bee** rounds in AI Hub sessions |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Title selection\n\nTASK.md asks for a playful riff on the source's name, picked by a real RNG from 50 silently brainstormed titles. Fifty riffs were written. None uses the trademark \"Dragon Quest\" verbatim. Examples of the kinds of riff: Wagon Quest Builders, Dragon Crest Bricklayers, Dragon Quarry Builders. One was picked with Python's `secrets.randbelow(50)`, which draws from OS entropy (`os.urandom`), not a seeded PRNG. Result: index 42, **\"Dragon Quilt Builders\"**.\n\nThe pick sets the whole art and writing direction: the world is a patchwork quilt. Every block is a fabric patch with stitched seams, the villain unravels things, tools are sewing tools, and puns are sewing puns.\n\nFile naming (fixed; do not improvise): GameTitle = `DragonQuiltBuilders`, SOURCE = `DragonQuestBuilders`.\n- Dev folder: `./DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174740/`\n- Deliverable: `./DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174740.html`\n- Exit interview: `./exit-interview.json`\n\nOn-screen title (logo, `<title>`, About screen, AI Hub name): **Dragon Quilt Builders**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Design pillars\n\n1. **Everything is a patch.** Each block is a 16-unit fabric square with stitched edges. Building feels like quilting: colourful, cosy and tactile. Breaking a block makes a satisfying *rrrip* and a burst of fabric scraps. Placing one makes a *stitch!* and a squash-in.\n2. **You are the only one with the Knack.** NPCs cannot build. Every bed, door and lantern you make visibly changes their lives: they use your rooms, sleep, eat, cook, help, and thank you. Make that cause and effect loud: popups, hearts, jingles.\n3. **Readable at ten feet.** Big chunky pixels (`res: 'ps1'` scaled to the TV), a few large UI elements, controller-first prompts, never dense web-style screens.\n4. **Warm, punny, earnest.** Dragon Quest-style writing: short lines, exclamation marks, gentle jokes, distinct voices, and a silent hero.\n5. **Data-driven and designer-ready.** Blocks, items, recipes, rooms, monsters, residents, quests, waves, songs and world stamps are all plain data tables. The building mechanic doubles as the level editor: dev mode exports any region you build as a stamp.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Deliverables, folder layout, commands\n\n```\n./PLAN.md, ./TASK.md, ./STAGE.md                      (given)\n./DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174740.html   (built; the game)\n./exit-interview.json                                  (written at the end; parses as JSON)\n./DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174740/\n    engine/            copy of my-3d2dge dist/handoff v0.85.2 (my-3d2dge.js is modified, see §4.2)\n    vendor/aihub/      session.js (unmodified client 1.4.0), harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (testing only)\n    src/               game modules, concatenated in file-name order (see §5.2)\n    src/index.template.html\n    tools/build.mjs    bundles everything into the root HTML; also writes dev/index.html\n    tools/hub-serve.mjs  serves a folder on 127.0.0.1:<OS port> for the hub harness (prints the port; kills only its own PID)\n    tests/run.mjs      THE test command (see §11)\n    tests/lib/*.mjs    headless loader, fake hub bus, autopilot\n    tests/unit/*.test.mjs, tests/play/*.test.mjs, tests/net/*.test.mjs, tests/browser/*.mjs\n    dev/index.html     loads engine + src files separately (debugging, file://)\n    check-output/      screenshots and filmstrips (git-ignored)\n```\n\nGetting the dependencies:\n- Do not clone into `/tmp`, which is a small shared tmpfs and nearly full. Work inside the dev folder.\n- Engine:\n  ```\n  git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git _eng\n  cd _eng\n  git sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\n  ```\n  Then copy `dist/handoff/*` into `engine/` and delete `_eng`.\n- Hub: `curl -fsSO` the four files from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/` (session.js, harness.html, bridge.js, round.js) into `vendor/aihub/`. Read `lib/hub/README.md` (the protocol) once, whole.\n- Inside `engine/`, run `npm install` (Playwright 1.63.0 is pinned) and `npx playwright install chromium`. Both are for the browser checks only.\n\nCommands (run from the dev folder):\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `node tools/build.mjs` | Writes the root HTML: `<meta charset>`, then **session.js inlined unmodified as the first script in `<head>`**, the hub meta tags, the boot splash, the engine, then `src/*.js` in order. Also writes `dev/index.html`. Fails on a missing file or a syntax error (`node --check` on each module first). |\n| `node tests/run.mjs` | Every headless suite. Prints **only failures** and one final line: `N passed, M failed (12.3 s)`. Exit code 1 on failure. |\n| `node tests/run.mjs --only voxel,rooms` | A subset (rerun only what the change covers). |\n| `node tests/run.mjs --browser` | Adds the Playwright suites: the AI Hub harness checks and the screenshot capture. Still one line of output plus failures. |\n| `node engine/tools/check.mjs <html>#<scene>` | The engine's browser checker (errors, look notes, snapshot). |\n| `node engine/tools/filmstrip.mjs <html>#play --steps \"...\" --seed 1` | Frame-by-frame strip, for judging animations. |\n\nPorts and processes: `tools/hub-serve.mjs` runs `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` and parses the printed port. It fetches the game page and checks that its `<title>` contains \"Dragon Quilt Builders\" before trusting anything. It kills only the PID it spawned. Never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`. Prefer `file://` and headless Node everywhere else.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Tech stack\n\n### 4.1 Stack\n- **my-3D2dge v0.85.2, full engine** (`engine/my-3d2dge.js`, which already contains every module: story, grid, songs, ...). Plain ES2020 JavaScript, no framework, no bundler beyond `tools/build.mjs` (string concatenation).\n- **Canvas 2D pixel rendering** through the engine's renderer. `res: 'ps1'` (320×240) with `expand: true`, so a 16:9 TV shows about 427×240 game pixels at a whole-number scale. GPU lighting is not used (it requires a TileMap); canvas lighting is.\n- **Audio:** the engine's chip synth and music sequencer, plus our own Web Audio formant voice synth (§6.17) on the engine's `AudioContext`.\n- **AI Hub Session Protocol v1**, client 1.4.0, shared-world mode (§6.21).\n- **Node 18+** for headless tests (`vm` contexts, as `engine/game/test.mjs` does). **Playwright** (pinned by the handoff) for the browser checks.\n- No external runtime dependencies and no CDN. The HTML must work from `file://`.\n- **Performance:** the budget is unlimited (TASK.md), so add detail, lights and effects freely. FPS targets and optimisation are out of scope. The atlas and culling design in §6.1 exists for correctness and a smooth feel, not as an optimisation target.\n\n### 4.2 Engine changes (keep a list; it goes into the About screen and the exit interview)\nEdit `engine/my-3d2dge.js` directly, with a `// DQB:` comment on every change:\n1. **Input:** an `input.wheelSteps()` accessor (whole notches this step, from `deltaY`) that works while `camera.wheel` is `false`, so the wheel can cycle the hotbar. A right-stick flick helper, `input.flick('x')`, which returns -1/0/1 once per push past 0.6 with a 0.25 s rearm.\n2. **Dialog:** an `onLetter(ch, index, page)` hook called for each newly shown character (default: the old single blip). Our voice synth uses it per speaker.\n3. **Audio:** a third bus, `audio._voiceBus` (master → voice), with `audio.voiceVolume` and a standard setting, \"Voices\" (0–100%).\n4. **Humanoid weapons:** new weapon kinds `'stick'`, `'club'` and `'ripper'`, drawn next to the existing `'sword'`/`'staff'` code using the same blade/tip joints, so `rig.tip()`, smears and moves keep working.\n5. **Glyph default:** `input.glyphs` defaults to `'playstation'` for this game. Our Settings item \"Button glyphs\" (PlayStation / Xbox) writes it.\n6. **Body ceilings:** none in the engine; `VoxelBody` (§6.1.6) handles them in game code. If the renderer, Body or Input need anything else, add it the same way and log it.\n\nDo **not** rebuild the standard features: pause menu, settings, rebinding, full screen, boot screen, save slots. Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: {...} })` and `game.saves`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Architecture\n\n### 5.1 Principles\n- **One namespace.** `globalThis.DQ` holds every module. Each `src/NN-name.js` is an IIFE that adds to `DQ` and reads only what earlier files defined.\n- **Logic and drawing are split.** Every system has `update(dt)` with no rendering, and a separate `draw(r)`. All logic runs headless in Node: `new E.Game()` is headless without a DOM and `draw` is skipped. UI screens are state machines (focus index, open tab, scroll), driven by input actions and testable headless; drawing reads that state.\n- **Determinism.** No `Math.random` in game logic. Use named seeded streams, `DQ.rng(label)` (built on `E.rng(seed ^ hash(label))`). World seed `20261103`. In a hub session, shared streams come from `AIHub.rng(label)`. Cosmetic randomness (particles, weather) uses its own streams.\n- **Event bus.** `DQ.events.on(name, fn)` and `emit(name, payload)` decouple systems. Quests, tutorials, the HUD, audio stingers, stats and tests all listen. Events:\n  - Blocks and items: `blockBroken`, `blockPlaced`, `objectPlaced`, `objectRemoved`, `itemGained`, `itemUsed`, `crafted`, `notion`\n  - Town: `roomFormed`, `roomLost`, `cheer`, `townLevel`, `patternPlaced`, `patternDone`\n  - Residents and combat: `npcArrived`, `talk`, `monsterKilled`, `builderHurt`, `builderKO`\n  - Story and time: `questStep`, `questDone`, `timeOfDay`, `siegeWave`, `bossPhase`, `chapterClear`\n- **One context object.** `DQ.ctx = { game, world, builder, town, story, quests, npcs, monsters, ui, audioX, tuning, events, net, mode }` is created per play session. Systems receive it in their constructor and never reach for globals beyond `DQ`.\n- **Data tables** live in `src/0x-data-*.js` as JSON-compatible literals: no functions in data except clearly marked `make` generators for stamps. Designers edit those files.\n\n### 5.2 Modules (`src/`, concatenated in this order)\n\n| File | Owns | Key API |\n|---|---|---|\n| `00-core.js` | `DQ` namespace, constants (`T=16`, world sizes), `hash`, `rng`, event bus, `DQ.ctx` factory, logging | `DQ.rng(label)`, `DQ.events` |\n| `01-data-blocks.js` | Block registry (§7.2) | `DQ.BLOCKS[id]`, `DQ.blockId(key)` |\n| `02-data-items.js` | Items, recipes, notions (§7.3–7.4) | `DQ.ITEMS`, `DQ.RECIPES` |\n| `03-data-objects.js` | Placeable objects and their `E.SHAPES` entries (§7.5) | `DQ.OBJECTS` |\n| `04-data-rooms.js` | Room recipes, town levels (§7.6–7.7) | `DQ.ROOMS`, `DQ.LEVELS` |\n| `05-data-world.js` | Regions, stamps (ASCII layers), POIs, portals, plant scatter, spawn tables (§7.1) | `DQ.WORLD` |\n| `06-data-npcs.js` | Residents: look, voice, schedule, job, dialog nodes (§7.8) | `DQ.NPCS` |\n| `07-data-monsters.js` | Monster and boss stats, AI params, drops (§7.9) | `DQ.MONSTERS` |\n| `08-data-quests.js` | Quests, steps, conditions, rewards, `E.Story` nodes, cutscene ids (§7.10) | `DQ.QUESTS`, `DQ.STORY_NODES` |\n| `09-data-audio.js` | Songs, jingles, sfx voices, voice profiles (§7.11–7.12) | `DQ.SONGS`, `DQ.SFX`, `DQ.VOICES` |\n| `09b-data-tuning.js` | Tuning defaults and slider metadata (§6.19) | `DQ.TUNING_DEFS` |\n| `10-voxel-world.js` | Voxel storage, edits, queries, collision, raycast, save diff | `DQ.VoxelWorld` (§6.1) |\n| `11-voxel-render.js` | Face-sprite atlas, column-run rendering, cutaway, water, void fringe, overlays | `DQ.VoxelRenderer` |\n| `12-worldgen.js` | Terrain, layers, ores, trees, water, stamps, plants, portals | `DQ.generateWorld(world, def)` |\n| `13-physics.js` | `VoxelBody` (ceilings, auto-hop, water, void), stair ramps | `DQ.VoxelBody` |\n| `14-input.js` | Action presets, intents, cursor modes, mouse picking, glyph icons and prompt strings | `DQ.Controls`, `DQ.Glyphs` |\n| `15-builder.js` | Player entity: stats, equipment, actions (swing, break, place, use, eat, interact), KO and respawn | `DQ.Builder` |\n| `16-inventory.js` | Bag, hotbar, chests, Town Chest, equipment, stacks, overflow | `DQ.Bag`, `DQ.Chest` |\n| `17-crafting.js` | Stations, recipe availability, crafting, notion unlocks | `DQ.Crafting` |\n| `18-objects.js` | Object instances: doors, lights, beds, stations, traps, ballistas, portals, plants (regrowth) | `DQ.Objects` |\n| `19-rooms.js` | Room detection and classification, room registry | `DQ.Rooms` |\n| `20-town.js` | Banner, border, Cheer, town level, Patterns (ghosts, progress, helpers) | `DQ.Town` |\n| `21-nav.js` | Voxel navigation graph, A*, flow fields, breaker paths | `DQ.Nav` |\n| `22-npcs.js` | Resident AI: schedule, needs, jobs, helping, fighting, Cheer bubbles | `DQ.Residents` |\n| `23-monsters.js` | Monster AI, spawning, despawning | `DQ.Monsters` |\n| `24-combat.js` | Damage, hit effects, knockback, i-frames, drops, the Lost Bundle | `DQ.Combat`, `DQ.Drops` |\n| `25-siege.js` | Siege director (prep, waves, fail/retry), boss directors | `DQ.Siege` |\n| `26-bosses.js` | Sir Buttonsworth, Gnarlgrim, Tangle mechanics | `DQ.Bosses` |\n| `27-time.js` | Day clock, lighting by hour, weather, sleep | `DQ.Clock` |\n| `28-story.js` | Quest director (steps on events), `E.Story` wrapper, cutscene scripts, objective targets | `DQ.Quests`, `DQ.Cutscenes` |\n| `29-ui-hud.js` | HUD, prompts, popups (Notion, Room, Level), Cheer, damage numbers, objective marker, cursor | `DQ.Hud` |\n| `30-ui-menus.js` | Title, name entry, Satchel (Bag/Map/Journal), crafting, chest, travel, banner, dialog skin, results, credits, About | `DQ.Menus` |\n| `31-audio.js` | Music director (per region, time and town level layers), sfx defs, Animalese voice synth | `DQ.AudioX` |\n| `32-save.js` | `game.saves` read/write, versioning, migrate, autosave | `DQ.Save` |\n| `33-dev.js` | Dev mode panel, cheats, sliders, overlays, stamp export | `DQ.Dev` |\n| `34-attract.js` | Attract-mode director (vignettes, AI builder, cheats) | `DQ.Attract` |\n| `35-ai.js` | Shared AI \"autopilot\" (walkTo, breakAt, placeAt, craft, talk, fight). Used by attract, tests and Bee bots | `DQ.Autopilot` |\n| `36-net.js` | AI Hub session flow, message codec, host and client logic, loopback | `DQ.Net` |\n| `37-bee.js` | Quilting Bee rules (hub round and solo challenge), its world, scoring | `DQ.Bee` |\n| `38-scenes.js` | Scenes: title, attract, newgame, intro, play, results, credits, bee, hubcard | `DQ.Scenes` |\n| `99-main.js` | Creates `E.Game`, settings, menu, boot steps, `game.debug`, starts | — |\n\n### 5.3 Game object and scenes\n```js\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'iso', views: ['iso', 'threequarter'],\n  input: DQ.Controls.PLAY, id: 'dragon-quilt-builders',\n  menu: { saves: { slots: 3, version: 1, migrate: DQ.Save.migrate },\n          items: [['Satchel', openSatchel], ['Dev Tools', DQ.Dev.open], ['About & Credits', openAbout]],   // Dev Tools hidden in a hub session\n          settings: DQ.SETTINGS, controls: DQ.Controls.NAMES, quit: 'title' } });\n```\n- **Scenes:** `title` (menu and attract countdown), `attract`, `newgame` (name, look), `intro` (story crawl), `play` (`pausable: true`, `camera: { zoom: false }`: the engine's continuous player zoom stays off, because our own zoom steps call `game.setZoom`, §6.1.3), `results` (chapter clear), `credits`, `about` (also openable over the pause menu), `bee` (Quilting Bee, solo or hub), `hubcard` (the game's own AI Hub card).\n- **Boot steps** (`game.start({ boot: { title: 'DRAGON QUILT BUILDERS', steps } })`):\n  1. \"Unrolling the quilt\" (world generation)\n  2. \"Threading the needles\" (face-sprite atlas, icons)\n  3. \"Waking the residents\" (nav graph)\n  4. \"Tuning the voices\" (audio definitions)\n\n  Boot messages: \"Counting the buttons\", \"Untangling yarn\", \"Ironing the seams\".\n- **`play.update(dt)` order:**\n  1. `Controls.intent()`\n  2. `Builder.update`\n  3. block actions\n  4. `Objects.update` (doors, traps, ballistas, plants)\n  5. `Residents.update`\n  6. `Monsters.update`\n  7. `Combat.resolve`\n  8. `Drops.update`\n  9. `Town.update` (rooms debounce, Cheer, Patterns)\n  10. `Quests.update`\n  11. `Clock.update` (spawns, weather)\n  12. `Siege.update`\n  13. camera (`game.focus` on the builder with lead)\n  14. `Hud.update`\n\n  A modal UI (dialog, menu, cutscene) short-circuits gameplay updates. The world still animates (particles, water) and the clock pauses.\n- **`play.draw(r)` order:**\n  1. void background\n  2. `VoxelRenderer.queue(r)` (sets `r._map`)\n  3. objects (`r.shape`)\n  4. Pattern ghosts\n  5. town border stitches\n  6. drops\n  7. actors (builder with `xray`, residents, monsters)\n  8. cursor\n  9. lights (`game.lights.add`)\n  10. `r.overlay` (HUD, popups, dialog, menus)\n- **`game.debug`:**\n  - `snapshot()` returns `{ scene, day, hour, builder: {x,y,z,hp,maxHp,belly,equip}, bag: {id:n}, town: {level, cheer, border, rooms:[type]}, quests: {id: step|'done'}, residents: [{id,x,y,state}], monsters: n, siege: {phase, wave}, boss: {id, hp, phase}, errors: n }`.\n  - Helpers: `teleport(x,y,z)`, `give(id,n)`, `flag(k,v)`, `beat(id)` (jump to a story beat), `world` (the VoxelWorld), `ctx`.\n\n---\n## 6. Systems\n\n### 6.1 Voxel world (the riskiest system; built first, see §9 MS1)\n\nThe engine's `TileMap` is a heightmap: one wall type per cell, no passages under things. Building needs real 3D blocks (roofs, bridges, tunnels, mixed-material walls), so the game has its own `VoxelWorld`. It plugs into the engine where the engine expects a map.\n\n#### 6.1.1 Data\n- Block size `T = 16` world units (one tile). Axes: x east, y south, z up.\n- Story world: `W = 144, D = 144` columns, `H = 24` levels. Bee world: `48 × 48 × 16`.\n- `vox: Uint8Array(W*D*H)`, column-major: `idx(x,y,z) = (y*W + x)*H + z`, so a column is contiguous.\n- `meta: Map<idx, {rot, hp}>` holds stair rotation and partial damage. Damage heals back after 4 s without hits.\n- `colVer: Uint32Array(W*D)` holds per-column versions for render caches. `topZ: Uint8Array(W*D)` is the highest solid level + 1, kept on edit.\n- **Objects** (furniture, doors, lights, plants) are *not* voxels. They live in `DQ.Objects` with an anchor cell `(x,y,z)` and occupy that cell (doors and portals occupy 2 levels). `world.objAt(x,y,z)` is indexed by `Map<idx, obj>`.\n- Block properties from `DQ.BLOCKS`: `solid`, `opaque`, `liquid`, `hp`, `tier`, `drop`, `tex`, `light`, `shape` (`'cube' | 'stair'`), `placeable`.\n- **Level 0 is Backing Cloth** (unbreakable). Below and outside the world is the **Unravel**, a void. Anything falling below z = −16 (through a void hole or off the world's edge) respawns at its last safe position after a 0.6 s \"unravel\" effect. The builder takes 4 damage.\n\n#### 6.1.2 API (`DQ.VoxelWorld`)\n```\nget(x,y,z) → id            set(x,y,z,id,{by, silent}) → bool (emits blockPlaced/blockBroken, bumps colVer of the column and 4 neighbours)\nsolidAt(x,y,z)  opaqueAt(x,y,z)  liquidAt(x,y,z)   (integer cells; outside the world and below level 0 is void, which reads as air)\ntopZ(x,y)                  standLevels(x,y) → levels L where solid(L-1) and air(L), air(L+1)\ngroundAt(px,py,r,z,step)   highest solid top ≤ z+step under the circle footprint, −Infinity over void (world units; feeds E.Body-style physics)\nceilingAt(px,py,r,z)       lowest solid bottom above z within the footprint (head bumps)\ncollide(body)              push a circle out of blocks overlapping [z+step, z+h) (same algorithm as TileMap.collide, per level)\nraycast(o,d,maxT) → {x,y,z, nx,ny,nz, t} | null          3D DDA (Amanatides–Woo) through cells\npickScreen(sx,sy) → {x,y,z, n:[nx,ny,nz], obj} | null     view ray through the screen pixel (see 6.1.5)\nbounds(view) → {x0,y0,x1,y1}                             camera clamp (as TileMap.bounds)\nT, solids=[] , _liftOrder(view,x,y,z,k)                  the renderer's hooks (see 6.1.4)\ndiff() → [[idx, id], ...]   applyDiff(list)               save and network: cells that differ from the generated base world\nhash() → u32                                              tests and net convergence\n```\nGeneration writes `base` (a copy of `vox` taken after `generateWorld`), so `diff()` is a cheap scan. Saves store only the diff (typically under 30 KB).\n\n#### 6.1.3 Rendering: face-sprite blitting per block, queued per column run\n- **Face atlas.** For each `(view id, zoom step)` the renderer pre-renders a small sprite for every material and visible face: `top`, `faceA` (the camera-facing side on the viewer's left) and `faceB` (right). In three-quarter view there is `top` and `front`. Each material gets 4 variants (picked by `hash(x,y,z) & 3`).\n  - Sprites are rasterized per pixel with `E.tones(base)`. The pattern functions are in §8.1. Each face is an exact parallelogram of the projected block face, snapped to whole pixels. All blocks of a view and zoom project to the same shapes, so one atlas serves the whole world. It is rebuilt only when the zoom step changes. A 90° turn keeps iso faces identical, and the renderer only swaps which world face maps to A/B.\n  - Light: top = `base`, faceA = `sh`, faceB = `deep`, following the engine's \"light from the viewer's upper left\".\n- **Zoom steps.** Zoom is quantized to `[0.75, 1, 1.25, 1.5, 2]` (default 1). Our own zoom actions (− / =, right stick ↑/↓) step through them with `game.setZoom(step)`. The play scene sets `camera: { zoom: false }`, so the engine's continuous player zoom stays off and the atlas only ever needs these five scales. The chosen step is remembered in our settings (`dqZoom`).\n- **Run queueing.** For each visible column, split its opaque solid blocks into runs `[z0, z1)`. Queue each run once:\n  ```js\n  r.queue(cx*16+8, cy*16+8, z0*16, g => drawRun(g, r, cx, cy, z0, z1), { occluder: true, top: z1*16 })\n  ```\n  `drawRun` blits, bottom to top, each block's visible faces: a side face shows where the neighbour column is not opaque at that level, and the top face shows where the block above is not opaque or is cut away. Then come overlays: crack stages on damaged blocks, contact AO (a 2-px dark band on side faces at a run's bottom where it meets the ground), top-face edge AO (a dark band along an edge whose neighbour is one level higher), and sun-shadow tint on tops that have a taller column within 2 cells toward the light. Overlays use `px.blend(g, a, 'multiply', ...)` with shared per-view overlay sprites.\n- **Visible columns.** Invert the four screen corners to the ground plane at `z = 0` and at `z = H*16`. Take the cell bounding box plus 2 cells of margin, then clamp it. That is about 500–700 columns at zoom 1.\n- **Liquids.** Water blocks are queued separately: `r.queue(..., z*16, drawWater)`. The water top is drawn with `px.blend(.7)` and a 4-frame shimmer, plus side faces next to air. Blocks under water get a blue multiply tint.\n- **Void and fringe.** Behind the world, draw a deep-navy gradient (`r.sky(['#06051a','#0d0b2a','#141238'])`) with drifting cream \"lint motes\" and a faint stitched grid. Edge columns and void holes get frayed fringe threads: 1-px lines hanging 1–2 blocks below the lowest block, swaying with time. These are queued just before their column.\n- **Cutaway** (seeing inside roofs, tunnels and canopies). Each frame, set `cut = heroLevel + 2` when an opaque block sits at a level in `[heroLevel+2, heroLevel+6]` in either of these places:\n  - the hero's own column;\n  - any of the 3 columns toward the camera. Step from the hero's cell by `(round(view.fx), round(view.fy))`, the ground direction that points at the camera; in iso both components are ±1.\n\n  Ordinary 2-high room walls (levels `heroLevel` and `heroLevel+1`) are never cut; the x-ray silhouette shows the hero behind them.\n\n  Blocks at `z ≥ cut` within radius 6.5 cells of the hero are skipped. A 1.5-cell band at the edge is drawn every other pixel (`px.polyDither`). A block just below the cut with a block above it gets a \"cross-section\" overlay: a darker top with a diagonal stitch hatch. The cut eases in and out over 0.2 s (the radius grows from 0). It stays on for 0.5 s after the hero leaves cover (hysteresis), so it does not flicker at doorways. Dev toggle: \"Cutaway: auto / always / off\".\n- **X-ray.** The builder, remote players and bosses use `r.actor(..., { xray: { color, actors: false } })`. They show as tinted silhouettes behind blocks (the engine does this through `occluder` queue items).\n- **Draw cost guard.** No baking hitches: blits are cheap. If a frame queues more than 2,500 runs (extreme zoom-out), draw the far columns' top faces only.\n\n#### 6.1.4 Depth sorting\nThe renderer sorts by `view.order(x,y,z) = fx*x + fy*y + z*1e-3`. Runs in one column sort bottom to top, and columns sort by depth. For things that stand on blocks or sit under overhangs, the renderer calls `r._map._liftOrder(view, x, y, z, k)` for every queued item above `z > .5`. `VoxelRenderer.queue` sets `r._map = world`. Call it before queuing any actor or object: the renderer reads `r._map` at queue time and resets it to null at the start of every frame. Implement both directions:\n- **Lift.** If the item's cell has a run whose top is `≤ z+1` and it is the highest such run, return `max(k, order(center, run.z0*16) + .002 + z*1e-5)`.\n- **Sink.** If the item's cell has a run with `z0*16 ≥ z + 20` (an overhang above its head), return `min(k, order(center, thatRun.z0*16) - .002)`.\n\nAlso provide `world.T = 16` and `world.solids = []` (the renderer reads both). Test with a diorama (§11.2): a hero under a roof, on a bridge with a body below, beside and behind 2- and 3-high walls, on stairs, and in a doorway. Write a filmstrip check in all 4 rotations.\n\n#### 6.1.5 Picking (mouse and touch-free targeting)\nUse the line of sight through a screen pixel, as `TileMap.pick` computes it: P and N from the view rows. Clip to the world box and run the 3D DDA from the camera side. The first opaque or solid block hit, with the entry face normal, is the **break target**. `hit + normal` is the **place target**. Objects take part through `world.objAt` (placed objects count as hit targets, so the mouse can click a bench or a bed). Actors are picked with the engine's `{ pick: entity }` on `r.actor`/`r.shape` and `r.pick(sx, sy)` (the last frame's draws). Actor picks win over blocks when both are under the pointer. Respect the cutaway: cut blocks are not pickable.\n\n#### 6.1.6 Physics (`DQ.VoxelBody extends E.Body`)\n- Builder body: `r = 5, h = 28` (fits under a 2-block ceiling), `eye = 22, chest = 16`, `step = 2`, `gravity = 800`.\n- **`update(dt, world)`.** Run `E.Body.update` (it calls `world.collide` and `world.groundAt`). Then check the ceiling: if `vz > 0` and `z + h > ceilingAt(...)`, clamp `z` and set `vz = 0`.\n  - **Stairs:** in a stair cell the ground is a ramp along the stair's `rot` (0 → 16 over the cell), so walking up stairs needs no jump.\n  - **Water:** in water, speed ×0.55. A column with water 2 or more levels deep blocks walkers like a wall (bodies cannot enter, and a falling body is pushed out to `lastSafe`). The builder cannot swim, as in the source.\n- **Auto-hop** (setting, on by default). Moving into a 1-block ledge with 2 free levels above it, on the ground, for over 0.08 s triggers `jump(215)` with a small forward boost.\n- **Safe positions.** Store `lastSafe` every 0.5 s while standing on solid ground that is not near a void edge.\n- **No block may be placed inside a body.** `Builder.place` checks every builder, resident and monster AABB first, then shows \"Something's in the way!\"\n- **`world.escape(body)`** moves a buried body to the nearest standable cell within 4 cells. It runs after any set() that could bury something (worldgen stamps, Tangle blocks).\n\n### 6.2 Camera and views\n- `game.follow(builder.body, { z: 14, lead: 18 })`, with `game.cam.bounds = v => world.bounds(v)` and `cam.smooth = .12`.\n- **Rotation:**\n  - Q/E keys, or a right-stick flick (`input.flick('x')`), turn by ±90° with `game.rotateView(±90)`.\n  - Cover the snap with a 0.18 s crossfade: grab the previous frame's buffer before turning and blend it out with `px.blend` on the overlay. Play a soft \"whoosh\" (`sfx('whoosh', {vol:.2})`).\n  - Movement keeps working in screen space (`view.screenDirToGround`).\n- **Zoom:** right stick ↑/↓ or `−`/`=` step through the zoom levels (§6.1.3). In play the mouse wheel belongs to the hotbar; it zooms only on the Map screen. R3/R resets rotation and zoom (`game.resetCamera()`, then zoom step 1).\n- **Settings → Camera view:** Isometric (default) or Three-quarter. It switches with `game.setView`; the atlas has entries for both.\n- Cutscenes take the camera with tweens on a proxy focus object and give it back on exit.\n\n### 6.3 The Builder (player)\n- **Look:** chosen at New Game. Look A: spiky hair and a bandana. Look B: ponytail and an apron. Four palette swatches. Name entry (default \"Patch\") on an on-screen letter grid that works with a pad, keys or mouse; typing letters works directly. The builder never speaks (Dragon Quest tradition); choices are shown as menu options.\n- **Rig:** `new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.0, build: 'chibi', ...look })`. Its head top must stay ≤ 28 units; assert in a test via `rig.worldOffset` of the head joint.\n- **Stats:**\n  - HP 40 (+10 per Heartpatch, ×3 in the story, so 70 max). Belly 100. Base attack 4.\n  - Equipment slots: weapon, body, shield.\n  - No experience levels. Growth comes from Heartpatches, gear and the town (as in DQB1).\n- **Movement (tunable):** walk 78, sprint ×1.45 (Belly drains ×1.5), accel 900, friction 10 when no input, jump 215, air control 0.6, coyote 0.08 s, jump buffer 0.12 s.\n- **Swing** (□ / LMB / J), using the equipped weapon:\n  - A tap swings a 2-hit combo (`E.Combo(['slash','backslash'], { window: .3 })`): 0.38 s per swing, active from 0.35 to 0.6 of it.\n  - Each swing hits monsters in a 70° arc within 22 units (`E.inArc` with `dz` ±14).\n  - If no monster is in the arc, the swing hits the **break target**:\n    1. It deals the weapon's `blockDmg`; the block's HP is `DQ.BLOCKS[id].hp`.\n    2. Crack overlays show at 33%, 66% and 90%.\n    3. At 0 HP the block breaks: fabric-scrap particles in its tones, a *rrrip* sfx, and the drop spawns.\n    4. Blocks above a tool's tier go *clink*: sparks, no damage, and the prompt \"Too tough for your Darning Stick!\"\n- **Spin Stitch** (unlocked in M5): hold swing for 0.6 s. A charge glow and rising tone play. Release for a 360° `spin` move: 1.5× damage, knockback 180, radius 26. It does not break blocks.\n- **Place** (△ / RMB / K), using the selected hotbar item:\n  - **Block:** set at the place target, if it is in reach (4.5 blocks from chest to cell centre), empty, not inside a body, and inside the world. The block pops in with a squash tween (scale 0.6 → 1.08 → 1 over 0.12 s), a stitch spark and a \"stitch\" sfx.\n  - **Object:** placed with facing = the builder's facing, snapped to 90°. A green or red ghost preview shows validity.\n  - **Usable** (food, Heartpatch, Banner): use it.\n  - A full hotbar is fine; place takes one item from the bag stack.\n- **Interact** (○ / F / RMB on an NPC or object): talk, open a chest, use a station, sleep, man a ballista, travel by portal, pick up the Lost Bundle, plant the Banner (on the marked spot).\n- **Eat:** use food from the hotbar or bag. Belly and HP restore (§7.3), with a munch ×3 animation.\n- **Hurt:** i-frames 0.8 s, knockback 90, flash, `expr: 'wince'`, a rumble of .35.\n- **KO** at 0 HP:\n  1. The `'die'` pose plays, then an *unravel* effect: the rig dissolves into thread particles.\n  2. Granny says \"Oh, dearie, you've come undone!\"\n  3. Fade, then respawn at the Banner (or the start point before it exists) with full HP and Belly 60.\n  4. A **Lost Bundle** stays at the KO spot (map marker) holding 50% (rounded down) of each material stack. Tools and key items are never lost. On Relaxed, nothing is lost. During the siege or boss, KO instead retries from the siege checkpoint (§6.12).\n- **Belly** drains 1 point every 9 s of real time (100 → 0 in 15 minutes); sprint ×1.5, fighting ×1.2. At 0, HP drops 1 every 4 s. \"Your belly is rumbling!\" warnings show at 25% and 10%.\n\n### 6.4 Input, controls, prompts (controller first, keyboard and mouse native)\n\nAction presets (`DQ.Controls.PLAY`, `DQ.Controls.MENU`) are passed to `game.input.use(...)`. The engine keeps the d-pad and arrow keys on movement and menus. The tables below are the defaults; players rebind through the standard Controls screen (`menu.controls` names them).\n\n| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Xbox glyphs | Keyboard / mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Move | Left stick / D-pad | LS / D-pad | WASD / arrows |\n| Camera turn 90° | Right stick ←/→ (flick) | RS | Q / E |\n| Zoom | Right stick ↑/↓ | RS | − / = |\n| Reset camera | R3 | RS click | R |\n| Jump (menus: confirm) | ✕ | A | Space (menus: Enter/Space) |\n| Swing / break (hold: Spin Stitch) | □ | X | Left click (keyboard-only: J) |\n| Place / use item | △ | Y | Right click (keyboard-only: K) |\n| Talk / interact (menus: back) | ○ | B | F (menus: Esc/Backspace) |\n| Hotbar previous / next | L1 / R1 | LB / RB | Wheel, 1–8, [ / ] |\n| Sprint (hold; toggle in Settings) | R2 | RT | Left Shift |\n| Builder's Eye (precision cursor, hold) | L2 | LT | C |\n| Satchel (Bag, Map, Journal) | Create | View | Tab / I (Bag), M (Map), N (Journal) |\n| Map shortcut | Touchpad click (button 17, if reported) | — | M |\n| Pause menu | Options | Menu | Esc / P |\n\n- **Cursor modes** (`Controls.cursorMode`):\n  - **Auto** (stick or keys used last). The target cell is the neighbour cell in the builder's facing, rounded to 8 directions.\n    - Break target: the first solid block in that cell at level L0 (feet), then L0+1, then L0−1.\n    - Place target: L0−1 if it is empty (fills holes), else L0, else L0+1, else L0+2. So walls 3 high can be stacked from the ground and pits get filled.\n  - **Mouse** (the mouse moved or clicked in the last 2 s). The target follows `world.pickScreen` under the pointer. LMB swings at that block and RMB places there. The builder turns to face the target.\n  - **Builder's Eye** (hold L2 / C). The builder stands still and a precision cursor appears at the auto target.\n    - Stick, d-pad or WASD step the cursor one cell (repeat .16 s), clamped to reach.\n    - R1 or Space raises the cursor one level; R2 or Shift lowers it.\n    - Swing and place act at the cursor.\n    - On screen: a grid of stitched dots around the cursor and a level number.\n- **Cursor display:** a stitched wireframe cube (12 projected edges, 1-px dashed cream thread, pulsing). The break target highlights the block's faces. The place target shows a 50%-alpha ghost of the held block or object. Out of reach, it turns red and dashed, with \"Too far\".\n- **Glyph icons** (`DQ.Glyphs.draw(g, actionOrCode, x, y)`). The engine font lacks ✕○□△, so the game draws its own 9×9 pixel icons:\n  - PlayStation: ✕ blue `#7aa7ff`, ○ red `#ff6a6a`, □ pink `#ff8ad8`, △ green `#55dca8`, on dark discs.\n  - Xbox: A green, B red, X blue, Y yellow letters on discs.\n  - L1/R1/L2/R2/LB/RB/LT/RT as rounded rectangles. Sticks as rings with L or R. Options/Create as pills.\n  - Keyboard keycaps are rounded light-grey rectangles with the key's label from `input.label()`; Space is wide.","numLines":400,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1675}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_013tXaMKQjaR6pQTq6DP2YjR","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
